Text and identity: read in full in the registered American Presidency Project transcription (jobs/app-executive-orders/output/texts/executive-order-8804-exemption-james-e-harper-from-compulsory-retirement-for-age.txt; grade mirror_transcription, acceptable_as_original false; standing note: original not yet held; replace and re-check quotes when an official copy is retrieved). Page check per jobs/claim-kit/AGENT_PROTOCOL.md (working-text paragraph), done 2026-11-04: the APP mirror page record with the same sha256 in jobs/app-executive-orders/output/MIRROR_DOCUMENTS.jsonl (Common Crawl capture 2025-10-13T10:08:50Z) has the heading "Executive Order 8804—Exemption of James E. Harper From Compulsory Retirement for Age", its date (1941-06-25) and subject agree with the held text, and its text_sha256 equals the held file's. Identity: the register lists this number with date 1941-06-25, signer Franklin D. Roosevelt and the title "Exemption of James E. Harper From Compulsory Retirement for Age"; the held text concerns James E. Harper and ends with the signature "FRANKLIN D ROOSEVELT" and the date line "June 25, 1941.". Clauses are quoted from that registered file, not from the unregistered clause-drafts copy.
Content: the order has one recital and one operative sentence. It exempts one named employee, James E. Harper (Chief, Employee Relations Section, Division of Personnel, Treasury Department), from compulsory retirement for age under section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), for a period of three months ending September 30, 1941. The recital says he "was exempted from compulsory retirement for age for periods totaling two years by Executive Orders No. 8139 of May 19, 1939, and No. 8422 of May 28, 1940" and asks that he "be further exempted from such compulsory retirement for a period of three months"; the earlier exemption orders it recites are earlier instruments, not later events, and not ends of this order. The inventory is therefore one clause, the exemption. Extension reading: this order is the further exemption; any later exemption of the same person would be a new exercise of the section 204 power with its own period and a later event, not an end of this order, and the packet's searches show none in the held texts.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"No earlier end found. The only cessation source is the order's own period, \"three months ending September 30, 1941\". The packet's bounded number search of the held order texts (lookup scope: exactly held_texts(), clause-drafts excluded) found no other held text that cites Executive Order 8804. The same search returned 2 hit(s); the status-history record for this order shows they are cross-reference notes to the earlier exemption orders recited in the text (EO 8139 and EO 8422), not later events. The person-name search of the held order texts (namier:held_texts_person_name, checked 2026-10-28, whose leads list later or undated orders only) found no later order naming James E. Harper. Statute check (the type rule research/readiness/type-rules/TR-RETIREMENT-EXEMPTION.json lists a change in the governing retirement statute as a possible earlier end): held APP exemption orders signed after this one still rest on the same section, for example EO 9271 of November 17, 1942 (\"section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), I hereby further exempt the said George T. Summerlin from compulsory retirement for age\") and EO 9530 of March 14, 1945 (\"section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), I hereby exempt the said Harry F. Hawley from compulsory retirement for age\"). Their wording shows that section 204 was still the authority for exemptions after this order's last day, so it had not been repealed. No held source shows that any amendment of section 204 extended or extinguished this order's dated exemption, and a later general rule is an end only with a reason it extinguished the command (owner ruling 2026-10-28). The employee's separation, retirement or death would end the practical use of the exemption but is not an instrument ending the command, and none is cited in the packet. Amendments are later events, not ends, and none is found.","independent_command_check":"None found. The order contains one command, the exemption of one named employee; it prescribes no report, procedure, delegation, appropriation or continuing duty. The compulsory-retirement rule it excepts comes from section 204 and the retirement statutes, which are independent of this order, so when the exemption's period ended no command of this order remained to continue.","savings_check":"The exemption is exhausted at 1941-09-30; its accrued effects (service, pay, retirement credit) are separate from the order and are stated as transferred_or_accrued. No later exemption order for the same person is found by the name search.","scope_check":"Scope: one named employee, James E. Harper (Chief, Employee Relations Section, Division of Personnel, Treasury Department). The dossier inventory has one whole-order clause (characters 0-722 of the registered file, the recital and the operative sentence); the signature and date line follow it in the full 757-character text. Nothing in the text is outside that clause, so no inventory correction is needed."}
